 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-10-24 (6): shoulder-surf: glides in from the vanilla third-person view, and the player no longer drifts onto the crosshair when strafing (written WITHOUT a toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen on a device; +2 tests in `render::shoulder`: `cargo test --lib shoulder` first; `python3 tools/shoulder_proto.py` runs both fixes in Python and passes).
+    - Order of views: first person -> vanilla third (4 blocks behind) -> shoulder surf, so the dolly out from the head is gone. The blend (`Rig::blend`, omega 16, about 0.3 s) now runs from the vanilla camera place to the shoulder shot: `desired = pivot - fwd * (THIRD + (dist - THIRD) * w)`, `THIRD = 4 + RADIUS` (the pull-in stops `RADIUS` short of the wanted place even in open air, so `4 + RADIUS` lands on vanilla's 4.0 to the block). The first frame also takes the pull-in fraction (`frac`) from the ray at once (`prime`), so a wall behind the player does not start the glide with the camera inside it. The field-of-view whip on the way in is removed (a 10 % whip over a 1.4 block slide was out of scale); the sprint / speed widening stays. `Shot::close` is no longer set at the start (the model is drawn from the first frame, like the vanilla view); it still is in a tight space.
+    - Strafing: the pivot's xz lag (up to the 0.6 cap) is along the strafe, so strafing toward the camera's shoulder (right shoulder, strafe right) took the whole 0.62 offset away and put the player on the crosshair. The lateral part of the lag that points toward the head's line is now dropped (the pivot's world x / z shifted along `right`); lag to the far side stays, and so does all the forward / back lag and the 0.6 cap. Not applied while the shoulders are swapping (`|side_s - side| < 0.05`), since the offset crosses the head then. Standing is unchanged: the player never gets nearer the crosshair than there. Python check: camera offset while strafing 0.02 before, 0.62 after.
+    - Tests: `the_glide_starts_where_the_vanilla_view_was`, `strafing_toward_the_shoulder_keeps_the_player_off_the_crosshair`.
+    - ponytail: the clamp is hard (no ease), so the camera's lateral velocity jumps when a strafe starts toward the shoulder; if it shows, ease it with a short spring on the clamped part. UNVERIFIED on a device: how much the player is off the crosshair standing (0.62 shoulder at 2.3 behind is about 15 degrees to the player's centre); if still too near, raise the `0.62` in `Rig::update`.
+    - Noticed, not changed: in open air the pull-in leaves the settled shot `RADIUS` (0.3) nearer than the numbers say (2.3 behind, not the 2.6 of entry (3); the ray is `len` long and `RADIUS` is taken off it). Raising the ray to `len + RADIUS` would restore 2.6; left alone because that is the look seen in the screenshots so far.
 - 2026-10-24 (5): shoulder-surf: faster dolly, smoother follow, no automatic swap (written WITHOUT a toolchain: not compiled, not run; one test replaced in `render::shoulder`).
     - Dolly: omega 8 -> 16 (about 0.3 s to the shoulder, no overshoot).
     - Smoother: pivot omega 16 / 26 -> 12 / 16 (xz / y); the pull-in is a fast ease (omega 30) instead of a snap, the release 8; the push-out and the eye fallback still catch what the ease leaves; the bank follows a smoothed turn rate (`Rig::turn`, omega 14), so a lumpy touch drag no longer jitters the roll.
